# UE 5.6 Chaos and Chaos Cloth MCP Surface Audit

Date: 2026-08-10
Target: Unreal Engine 5.6.1 and the `UnrealMCP` editor plugin

This audit records the native Chaos and Chaos Cloth APIs selected for the
Tivret MCP surface. The engine source was inspected read-only. UnrealMCP remains
an editor authoring bridge; authored levels, cloth assets, and Dataflow assets
must continue to load, cook, and run without UnrealMCP enabled.

## Engine source boundary

The selected public UE 5.6 headers are:

- `Chaos/ChaosSolverActor.h` and `ChaosSolverConfiguration.h` for
  `AChaosSolverActor`, `FChaosSolverConfiguration`, and destruction settings;
- `ChaosClothAsset/ClothAsset.h` for `UChaosClothAsset` collections and build
  lifecycle;
- `ChaosClothAsset/ClothComponent.h` for `UChaosClothComponent` configuration,
  property collections, proxy recreation, geometry scale, and parallel-task
  synchronization;
- `ChaosClothAsset/ClothAssetInteractor.h` for the reflected string-key cloth
  property accessors;
- `Chaos/CollectionPropertyFacade.h` for property enumeration, weighted
  low/high values, strings, and Enabled, Animatable, Legacy, Interpolable,
  Intrinsic, Dirty, and StringDirty flags;
- `ChaosClothAsset/CollectionClothFacade.h` for simulation/render topology,
  seams, fabrics, weight maps, gravity, air damping, and timestep;
- `ChaosClothAsset/ClothSimulationProxy.h` for atomic runtime metrics such as
  cloth/particle counts, iterations, substeps, linear-solve state, simulation
  time, and teleport state.

The corresponding implementation paths were also checked, especially
`Runtime/Experimental/Chaos/Private/PBDRigidsSolver.cpp` for
`ApplyConfig()`/`ApplyCVars()`, the Chaos Solver actor implementation for the
editor property-change route, and the Chaos Cloth Asset component/interactor
sources for collection lifetime, proxy reconstruction, and Dataflow
regeneration behavior.

The UnrealMCP build-side native implementation requires the `ChaosCloth`,
`ChaosClothAsset`, `ChaosClothAssetEngine`, `ClothingSystemRuntimeInterface`,
and `DataflowCore`/`DataflowEngine` modules rather than relying on transitive include
visibility. It does not directly use a `DataflowSimulation` type. The engine
plugins `ChaosCloth`, `ChaosClothAsset`, `ChaosClothAssetEditor`, and `Dataflow`
must be enabled for the editor module that hosts the native routes.

## Exposure decision

Generic actor/component reflection is adequate for ordinary reflected scalar
properties, but it is not the right contract for managed cloth collections,
collection facades, native simulation proxies, or Dataflow graph ownership.
Those areas need a narrow native JSON route because the MCP boundary must also
enforce unique target resolution, allowlisted fields, optimistic concurrency,
thread synchronization, reconstruction rules, saving, and independent
readback.

The accepted solver/modern-cloth surface is exactly eight commands:

| Command | Effect | Native purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `inspect_chaos_solver_settings` | Read | Inspect bounded solver matches and optional runtime state. |
| `configure_chaos_solver_settings` | High-risk write | Apply allowlisted solver settings to one exact actor. |
| `inspect_chaos_cloth_asset` | Read | Inspect cloth collections, property metadata, and Dataflow ownership. |
| `inspect_chaos_cloth_simulation_mesh` | Read | Page native simulation vertices, topology, and selected weight-map values. |
| `inspect_chaos_cloth_component` | Read | Inspect one or more cloth components and available runtime metrics. |
| `configure_chaos_cloth_component` | High-risk write | Change existing cloth properties and reviewed component settings. |
| `configure_chaos_cloth_dataflow_node` | High-risk write | Change allowlisted values on one exact cloth Dataflow node. |
| `configure_chaos_cloth_weight_map` | High-risk write | Replace or fill one guarded Dataflow-authored simulation weight map. |

The four inspectors are non-mutating. The four configurators default to
`dry_run=true`, require search-dispatch confirmation for apply mode, and use
strict independent readback.

## Solver contract

The reviewed, runtime-effective solver configuration includes:

- position, velocity, and projection iterations;
- collision cull distance, maximum push-out velocity, and initial-overlap
  depenetration velocity;
- clustering connection factor and union method;
- per-advance break and reschedule limits, clustering particle-release
  throttling minimum/maximum, and runtime-memory optimization;
- collision, break, and trailing data generation plus their filter settings.

`CollisionMarginFraction` and `CollisionMarginMax` exist in
`FChaosSolverConfiguration`, but UE 5.6 `FPBDRigidsSolver::ApplyConfig()` does
not apply them to the live solver. Inspection may report those stored values
with an explicit not-runtime-applied limitation; configuration must not claim
that changing them updates live simulation.

The same UE 5.6 function calls `ApplyCVars()` after applying actor values.
Non-negative values in the six reviewed global CVars can therefore override
position/velocity/projection iterations, collision cull distance, maximum
push-out velocity, and initial-overlap depenetration velocity. Inspection
reports their raw values, active state, and source-labelled inferred effective
values. This is a read-only, fixed-name diagnostic surface; it is not an
arbitrary console-variable API, and it does not write global process state.

Mutation accepts only the explicit native allowlist. It does not expose an
arbitrary property path or arbitrary Chaos console-variable setter. An empty
selector is acceptable only for bounded inspection; a write requires one exact
actor. Durable editor-world mutation is blocked in PIE/SIE, and `save_level`
defaults to false. Native JSON parsing is exact even through raw batch
subcommands: booleans, strings, numbers, objects, and arrays are not coerced,
and integer selectors are validated for finiteness, exact integral form, and
range before conversion. No-op solver/component patches do not notify,
transact, dirty, or save a pre-existing dirty target package, and both nested
and top-level mutation evidence reports no attempt. Actual save requests require
a clean exact target package so unrelated dirty editor work cannot be committed.
Successful editor-world Solver writes register an Undo/Redo callback that
re-applies the restored `Properties` struct to the live solver. Enqueue evidence
is true only when `AChaosSolverActor::GetSolver()` is live. The callback sends a
direct solver command rather than another actor property-change event, avoiding
duplicate construction-script/component re-registration on Undo.

## Cloth component contract

Inspection can enumerate the component's model/LOD property collections and
read the available `FClothSimulationProxy` metrics. Runtime reads use the
thread-safe proxy getters. Before mutation or reconstruction, the handler must
complete any existing parallel cloth simulation on the game thread.

Property updates address existing keys only and carry an audited value type;
the bridge must not infer bool, int, float, vector, or weighted-float semantics
from the managed collection's vector-backed storage. Writes use the exact
collection facade key instead of `UChaosClothAssetInteractor`'s private alias
fan-out, so validation, mutation, readback, and rollback have the same target
set. Intrinsic properties require `allow_intrinsic_recreate=true` only when the
requested value differs. `recreate_simulation="auto"`
reconstructs only when the accepted changes require it. Private component
fields are not writable merely because native code can reach them; public
component APIs remain the preferred mutation path.

Runtime collection updates are transient component-local overrides and are not
made durable by `save_level`. Level saving applies only to serialized component
settings; `teleport` and `teleport_and_reset` are one-shot actions. A requested
save with no persistent component setting fails closed instead of saving the
level without a durable target change. Proxy recreation checks
`p.ClothPhysics` before any mutation. One-shot actions run only after all
fallible readback, reconstruction, and save stages. Failure restores persistent
settings, each targeted property's exact raw Low/High vectors, string and flags,
teleport state, the original present/absent proxy state, and dirty/save state
when each phase can be verified. Recreating a previously existing proxy destroys
live particle and velocity state for which UE 5.6 has no public restore API;
presence may be rebuilt, but that failure path is deliberately reported as an
incomplete rollback through `rollback_simulation_state_restored=false`,
`partial_target_modified=true`, and `do_not_retry=true`. Component Undo/Redo intentionally
uses UE's existing `UActorComponent::PreEditUndo`/`PostEditUndo` re-registration;
`UChaosClothComponent::OnRegister` rebuilds the proxy and its derived collision
and teleport state, so a second MCP transaction callback would double-reset the
simulation.

That built-in Undo covers serialized component settings only. For a mixed
persistent/runtime command, component re-registration resets transient runtime
overrides and live simulation rather than restoring their pre-command values;
the response states that scope instead of implying full transient Undo.

Solver, component, and Dataflow writes detect an active `execute_batch`
transaction and join it. Only standalone route-owned transactions may be
cancelled during rollback; this preserves the batch-wide one-Undo contract even
when a later subcommand fails after an earlier success.

Solver and component rollback restore the package's original dirty state only
after serialized readback succeeds. A partial serialized rollback remains dirty
so the editor cannot silently discard it. Dataflow reports verified in-memory
property/regeneration restoration separately from durable rollback saving and
never saves a semantically incomplete rollback. Its durable save evidence also
requires both exact packages to be clean after the save call returns. Transient
objects or outer chains and transient/temp/memory/PIE/compiled-in packages are
rejected before a requested persistent mutation.

`recreate_simulation="always"` is an explicit live hard reset and therefore
reports `changed=true` even for same-value settings. Dataflow save requests use
the same clean-package preflight for both the graph and selected cloth asset.

## Cloth asset and Dataflow contract

`inspect_chaos_cloth_asset` reports both generated cloth collections and their
Dataflow owner. Direct edits to generated collection internals are not a
durable authoring strategy because a later Dataflow evaluation can overwrite
them. Durable authoring therefore uses
`configure_chaos_cloth_dataflow_node` and regenerates the cloth asset from its
source graph.

`inspect_chaos_cloth_simulation_mesh` closes the UE 5.6 Python exposure gap by
reading compiled solver positions/indices from `FChaosClothSimulationModel` and
same-index weight maps from `FCollectionClothConstFacade` natively. Its bounded
vertex/face pages can include positions, normals, triangle indices, and one
weight map without placing unbounded collection arrays in a single JSON
response. The response also emits the full LOD counts, available map names,
Dataflow/node identity, canonical MD5 guards, and an independent count/MD5 match
against each array copied into the compiled solver model. `topology_md5` covers
the complete simulation topology rather than only the returned page, so it can
safely round-trip into a later mutation.

Direct mutation of a generated collection would be overwritten on the next
Dataflow evaluation. `configure_chaos_cloth_weight_map` therefore targets one
exact current node type, `FChaosClothAssetWeightMapNode`, accepts exactly one of
an explicit normalized `[0,1]` float array or a normalized constant fill,
regenerates the selected cloth asset, and independently reads the generated
runtime collection back. Apply mode requires the exact non-empty node type,
Dataflow asset path, inspected vertex count, `expected_topology_md5`, generated
`expected_weight_map_md5`, and `expected_node_state_md5`; all fail closed on a
stale inspection. Those inspection values authorize only a dry-run. Apply also
requires `expected_path_md5` copied from a fresh writer dry-run using the same
target and requested values, so a changed source GUID, hop identity/order,
active/frozen state, semantic input source, or terminal path fails concurrency
before mutation. Mutation additionally requires exactly one passthrough-linked
Collection path from that node to the selected cloth terminal and requested LOD;
internal fan-out, an unrelated collection input, or another terminal/LOD is
rejected. A `MaxDistance` or `FixedEndWeightMap` consumer additionally requires
a recognized active, unfrozen downstream generator semantic path. The reviewed
legacy `MaxDistance` accepts Simulation Default Config only for the canonical
map when its input has no existing property schema. Modern `MaxDistance` requires
Simulation Max Distance Config followed by Long Range Attachment Config v2,
with custom generation disabled and `FixedEndSet` connected directly to that
selected node's `KinematicVertices3D` output. The legacy fixed-end path must
reach its reviewed Long Range Attachment generator. Unknown, remesh,
disabled/frozen, and custom derived-data routes fail closed because matching map
arrays alone do not prove that tether or kinematic solver inputs were rebuilt.
`require_runtime_binding=true` fails closed unless the named map is
property-consumed and present with identical count/MD5 in both the generated
LOD and compiled solver array.

For tether-affecting maps, independent readback canonicalizes each final
collection and compiled-solver tether as `(dynamic vertex, kinematic vertex,
reference-length bit pattern)`, sorts the tuples, and requires matching vertex
count, tether count, and MD5. When the reviewed path produces a kinematic
selection, the `KinematicVertices3D` collection selection and the compiled
solver's corresponding `VertexSets` entry are independently canonicalized as
sorted unique indices and must also match by count and MD5.
Apply verification runs map, semantic-path, tether, and kinematic-selection
checks after regeneration. A failure restores the node, regenerates again, and
requires the same tether and selection checks to match the captured pre-mutation
state before rollback can be reported successful. Apply and rollback therefore
cannot pass on collection-map equality alone.
Save remains opt-in, and failed apply/regeneration/readback/save paths attempt a
verified node-state restore and cloth regeneration before reporting rollback
status.

The Dataflow command requires an exact cloth asset and node name. Apply mode
also requires the exact non-empty `expected_node_type` and
`expected_dataflow_asset_path` returned by inspection and requires
`regenerate_asset=true`; `expected_current` remains optional. These values
fail closed when the graph changed after inspection. Connected top-level or
nested inputs are protected by `allow_connected_inputs=false`; explicit opt-in
reports the exact connected property-chain paths. An actual Dataflow change is
blocked when the asset is referenced by any other loaded/unsaved owner or by
another on-disk package because this route does not regenerate or verify fan-out
owners. A canonical same-value apply returns a no-op before that mutation gate
and performs no transaction, regeneration, dirtying, or save.
Regeneration defaults to
true, while package saving remains opt-in through `save_assets=false`.

After a failed apply, regeneration, readback, or save, the native handler
attempts to restore the selected node properties from the canonical
pre-mutation export text, regenerates the cloth asset, verifies the restored
values, and re-saves the restored packages if the original attempt reached
saving. This is reported as a semantic restore: regeneration can replace
derived data such as `AssetGuid`, so unsaved restored packages remain dirty and
the editor transaction is retained as the byte-exact manual undo path. A failed
rollback retains that transaction as well.

Cloth Dataflow node structs are editor-only and are not consistently exported
for safe direct linking. Generic node reflection therefore uses the node's
typed script struct, a Chaos-Cloth node classification gate, and a reviewed
top-level `FProperty` type allowlist. Generic `FDataflowNode` base fields,
arrays, maps, object properties, transient/deprecated fields, and
instance-disabled fields are excluded. A connected input is authoritative over
a local property value, which is why connected inputs fail closed by default.
Finite-number and hard `ClampMin`/`ClampMax` checks run recursively; bounds on
an outer weighted-value struct are inherited by its numeric leaves.

Every explicit asset path and nested value intended for persistence passes the
MCP dependency guard before native dispatch. The handler must never persist an
UnrealMCP class, module, function, object, or asset reference into the cloth or
Dataflow result.

## Mutation and error contract

Each configurator returns `mutation_result_v1`, including:

- resolved dry-run/apply mode;
- `before`, `after`, and `delta`;
- whether mutation was attempted, performed, and changed state;
- regeneration and save evidence where applicable;
- `verification.status`, method, checks, and post-mutation native readback.

Optimistic-concurrency mismatches, ambiguous targets, duplicate or overlapping
runtime LOD updates, unsupported node or property types, connected-input
protection, shared-graph protection,
regeneration failure, save failure, and readback failure return structured
errors. A native call that reports execution success without a passing strict
postcondition is normalized to failure by the Python policy layer.
Solver/component saves occur only after their native readback and proxy
postconditions pass; Dataflow saves occur only after node readback and cloth
regeneration pass.
Solver/component persistence targets the actual actor/component package, so a
streaming-sublevel target is not falsely reported saved after saving only the
owning persistent world. Each exact target package is checked for a clean dirty
state after saving.

## Deliberately excluded surface

- Chaos CVar writes or reads outside the six fixed solver-override diagnostics;
- arbitrary UObject/property-path mutation;
- creation of unknown collection property keys;
- direct `const_cast` mutation of `UChaosClothAsset` collections;
- broad Dataflow graph topology editing or arbitrary node creation;
- automatic mutation of shared Dataflow assets;
- runtime-module or gameplay dependencies on UnrealMCP;
- the deprecated Experimental Chaos Cloth Editor surface.

## Offline acceptance

The Python catalog baseline is 397 tools in 21 categories, with 23 tools in
`chaos`. Required offline checks are:

```powershell
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\smoke_tool_registration.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_chaos_tools_response_schema.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_tool_policy_contract.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\test_tool_discovery_contract.py
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\mcp_tool_exposure_benchmark.py check
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\mcp_tool_parity_audit.py --fail-on issues
uv --directory .\Python run python scripts\analysis\run_response_schema_tests.py
```

Parity must contain all eight exact Python/native names with no alias. Live
mutation verification is a separate opt-in isolated-editor gate and must not
reuse or save the user's current dirty map.
